    <h2 id="roles">Who is responsible for your data</h2>
    <p>When you take or run an exam, your institution decides why and how your data is used: it is the controller, and
        MindAttic processes the data on its behalf. Questions about your exam data, or requests to see or delete it, go to
        your institution first; we help them answer. For this website and its contact form, MindAttic is the controller.</p>

    <h2 id="exams">What is collected for an exam, and for how long</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Why</th><th scope="col">Kept</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Account: user name, email, password hash, two-step verification secret</td><td>Signing in</td><td>While the account exists</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Organization membership and role</td><td>Deciding what you can see and do</td><td>While you are a member</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Your answers (the transcript), word timings and speech measurements</td><td>Judging the answer, and the instructor's review</td><td>Your institution's retention period (90 days unless it sets another), then the words are removed</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Answer audio</td><td>Converted to text, and to the voice measurements below, in real time</td><td>Not stored: streamed to the speech recognizer and discarded</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Judgments, report and grade</td><td>Your result</td><td>With the exam record, until your institution or you ask for it to be deleted</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Integrity notes, derived only (face count, where you were looking, leaving the exam tab, a second voice)</td><td>Context for a human reviewer</td><td>With the exam record; detail removed with the transcript</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Video frames</td><td>None</td><td>Never leave your device and are never stored</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Consent record: the time, and a fingerprint of the exact notice you saw</td><td>Proof of what you agreed to</td><td>With the exam record</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>When the retention period ends, the outcome is kept and what you said is removed. Before every exam you see a
        plain notice of what is recorded, and you choose whether to go ahead.</p>

    <h2 id="camera">The camera</h2>
    <p>Your camera stays on for the whole exam: if it is refused, blocked or lost partway through, the exam pauses until it
        is working again, rather than carrying on without it. Camera checks run inside your browser. They work out, in
        memory, things like whether a face is in view, whether there is a second face, roughly where you are looking, and
        whether you are looking at the examiner while you answer. Only a short description of each event is sent: its type,
        when it started and ended, a confidence and one sentence. No frame, image, facial landmarks or face signature is
        sent or kept. These notes are low-confidence context for a reviewer, never a penalty, and they never decide your
        grade. Nothing in the camera checks infers emotion or deception.</p>

    <h2 id="voice">Your voice</h2>
    <p>Your spoken answers go to a speech-to-text service so the examiner can understand them. The audio itself is not
        stored: it is streamed to that service in real time and discarded.</p>
    <p>Where your institution allows it, your browser also measures a few summary numbers about your voice in each answer
        (pitch, loudness and similar). These are compared only with your own first answers in the same exam. Reviewers see
        the raw differences with a reminder that nerves, effort or a hard question change anyone's voice. The comparison
        names no emotion, is never part of your grade and is hidden from graders marking blind. It may make the examiner
        ask a further question.</p>
    <p><strong>In the EU and EEA these measurements are off by default</strong>, for organizations there and for students
        who live there, and an administrator can turn them off for everyone. Each change is recorded.</p>

    <h2 id="site">This website</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>No analytics, advertising or tracking.</strong> The public pages load nothing from other sites.</li>
        <li><strong>Cookies.</strong> Only the ones the service needs to work: your sign-in session, protection against forged
            form posts, and which organization you are working in. None is used to follow you across sites.</li>
        <li><strong>The contact form.</strong> We store what you type (name, work email, institution, role, what you asked for
            and your message) to reply to you. We don't store your network address with it. We keep it while we are in
            touch and delete it when you ask.</li>
        <li><strong>Server logs.</strong> Our hosting keeps technical logs to run and secure the service. They carry record
            identifiers, never your answers or passwords.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="sharing">Who else sees your data</h2>
    <p>People in your institution with the right role: your instructors, examiners and reviewers. And these service providers, which process data for us:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>AI model providers</strong> used by the examiner and scoring panel (such as Anthropic, OpenAI, Google and
            DeepSeek, as configured). They receive your answers for judging; never video or account details.</li>
        <li><strong>ElevenLabs</strong>, for the examiner's voice and for speech recognition.</li>
        <li><strong>Microsoft Azure</strong>, for hosting and the database.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
    <p>Depending on where you live, you may have the right to see the data held about you, have it corrected or deleted,
        object to some uses, or complain to a data protection authority. Your institution can export everything held about
        you in its organization, and can erase your exam records. Ask your institution first; if you can't reach them, use
        our <a href="contact">contact form</a> and we will pass your request on.</p>

    <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
    <p>If we change how data is used, we will update this notice and the date at the top. The notice shown before each exam
        is fingerprinted, so a change to its wording is visible in the record.</p>
</div>
